Hands On Lab — Subqueries & Nested Queries (12 Tasks)



Assumed Tables

-- products(product_id, product_name, category, unit_price)

-- sales(sale_id, product_id, qty, discount_pct, sale_date, store)



Note: Use only subqueries/nesting here (no JOIN in SELECT list unless specified). MySQL 5.7/8.0 safe. Dates as 'YYYY-MM-DD'.



Task 1 — Scalar subquery in WHERE

Problem: Return sales whose qty is strictly above the overall average qty.

SELECT *

FROM sales

WHERE qty > (SELECT AVG(qty) FROM sales)

ORDER BY sale_id;



Task 2 — Scalar subquery in SELECT (derived column)

Problem: Show each sale with the overall average unit price of all products as a separate column.

SELECT

 sale_id, product_id, qty, discount_pct, sale_date, store,

 (SELECT AVG(unit_price) FROM products) AS avg_unit_price_all

FROM sales

ORDER BY sale_id;



Task 3 — Subquery with IN (category filter)

Problem: Return sales whose product belongs to Stationery.

SELECT *

FROM sales

WHERE product_id IN (

 SELECT product_id FROM products WHERE category = 'Stationery'

)

ORDER BY sale_id;

 

Task 4 — Subquery with NOT IN (exclude a category)

Problem: Return sales not in category Electronics.

SELECT *

FROM sales

WHERE product_id NOT IN (

 SELECT product_id FROM products WHERE category = 'Electronics'

)

ORDER BY sale_id;



Task 5 — Correlated subquery (per-store above-average qty)

Problem: For each store, return rows whose qty is > store’s own average qty.

SELECT *

FROM sales s

WHERE qty > (

 SELECT AVG(qty) FROM sales WHERE store = s.store

)

ORDER BY store, sale_id;

Task 6 — EXISTS for category membership

Problem: Return sales for which the product exists in products with unit_price > 1000.

SELECT *

FROM sales s

WHERE EXISTS (

 SELECT 1

 FROM products p

 WHERE p.product_id = s.product_id

   AND p.unit_price > 1000

)

ORDER BY sale_id;

  

Task 7 — NOT EXISTS to find “orphan” product_ids

Problem: List products that have never been sold.

SELECT *

FROM products p

WHERE NOT EXISTS (

 SELECT 1 FROM sales s WHERE s.product_id = p.product_id

)

ORDER BY product_id;



Task 8 — ANY/SOME (comparison to set)

Problem: Find products whose price is greater than ANY unit price of Stationery items (i.e., greater than the minimum Stationery price).

SELECT *

FROM products

WHERE unit_price > ANY (

 SELECT unit_price FROM products WHERE category = 'Stationery'

)

ORDER BY unit_price DESC, product_id;



Task 9 — ALL (comparison to entire set)

Problem: Find products whose price is greater than ALL unit prices of Stationery (i.e., greater than the maximum Stationery price).

SELECT *

FROM products

WHERE unit_price > ALL (

 SELECT unit_price FROM products WHERE category = 'Stationery'

)

ORDER BY unit_price DESC, product_id;

 

Task 10 — Subquery for “top price per category” list

Problem: Return each product that matches the maximum unit_price of its category.

SELECT *

FROM products p

WHERE unit_price = (

 SELECT MAX(unit_price)

 FROM products

 WHERE category = p.category

)

ORDER BY category, product_id;



Task 11 — Semi-join via IN: sales only for “premium” products

Problem: A premium product is price ≥ 5000. Return those sales.

SELECT *

FROM sales

WHERE product_id IN (

 SELECT product_id FROM products WHERE unit_price >= 5000

)

ORDER BY sale_id;



Task 12 — Anti-join via NOT IN: sales of non-discounted categories

Problem: Suppose Accessories category is not allowed today. Return sales whose product is not in Accessories.

SELECT *

FROM sales

WHERE product_id NOT IN (

 SELECT product_id FROM products WHERE category = 'Accessories'

)

ORDER BY sale_id;
